     <article class="story single" data-beat="environment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENVIRONMENT</span> · Guardian Environment · 2026-09-30 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Approval of biggest coal project in NSW planning commission’s history described as ‘infamous betrayal’</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#environment</span> <span>#coal mining</span> <span>#New South Wales</span> <span>#Independent Planning Commission</span></div>
       <p>The Independent Planning Commission (IPC) in New South Wales has given its approval to the Hunter Valley Operations (HVO) Continuation Project, a decision that greenlights the largest coalmine project ever presented to the commission. This contentious assessment will allow mining to persist at the Hunter Valley site until 2045. The IPC concluded that the climate change impacts stemming from the project would be 'outweighed' by its anticipated economic benefits. This ruling has ignited strong reactions, with climate and environment groups vocally describing the approval as an 'infamous betrayal' and labelling the project a 'carbon bomb'. The assessment process itself was extensive, drawing in a wide array of stakeholders including unions, state authorities, and generating more than 10,000 public submissions. The approval underscores the complex balance regional authorities must strike between industrial output and environmental stewardship. With mining slated to continue for over two decades, the long-term implications for the Hunter Valley and broader climate goals are now a critical point of focus. The decision sets a significant benchmark for future resource development in the region, highlighting the persistent debate between economic imperatives and ecological concerns.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-30/approval-of-biggest-coal-project-in-nsw-planning-commissions.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>What to watch: The ongoing monitoring of environmental impacts and economic outcomes in the Hunter Valley.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately reflects the provided source summary without any embellishment.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/sep/30/hunter-valley-coal-project-singleton-ipc-planning-decision" rel="noopener">Guardian Environment</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
