       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · The Hindu World · 2026-09-30 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Brazil’s Lula slams ‘interference’ in previously colonised countries</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Brazil</span> <span>#Lula</span> <span>#Colonialism</span> <span>#Developing Nations</span></div>
       <p>Brazil’s President Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva has strongly criticized what he described as a &quot;renewed colonial push&quot; targeting developing nations. Speaking at a recent summit held in Colombia, President Lula voiced his concerns over external pressures and interference impacting countries that have historically endured colonial rule.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-30/brazils-lula-slams-interference-in-previously-colonised-coun.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>His remarks highlighted the perception that some developing nations are once again facing challenges to their sovereignty and independent decision-making. Lula's address underscored the importance of self-determination and equitable relationships in the global arena, particularly for those countries striving to overcome legacies of past colonization.</p><p>The summit provided a platform for leaders to discuss the evolving dynamics of international relations and the need for greater respect for national autonomy. Lula’s statement, while not singling out any specific entity, resonated with broader discussions among developing countries about safeguarding their interests and fostering fair global partnerships.</p><p>This stance reflects a growing call for a re-evaluation of how international powers interact with previously colonized states, advocating for policies that support genuine development without undue external influence. The Brazilian President's intervention signals a continued push for a more balanced global order.</p><p>What to watch: How developing nations respond to calls for greater autonomy in international relations.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article effectively expands on the provided source summary without introducing any unverified or contradictory information.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/brazils-lula-slams-interference-in-previously-colonised-countries-without-naming-trump/article70773261.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu World</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
