       <h1>India will get better with time, says Abhishek Nayar on bowling ahead of 2nd ODI vs West Indies</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#India cricket</span> <span>#Abhishek Nayar</span> <span>#West Indies</span> <span>#ODI</span></div>
       <p>Abhishek Nayar has expressed confidence in India's bowling unit, stating that the team will improve with time. His comments come ahead of the second One Day International against West Indies, following a challenging performance in the first ODI.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-30/india-will-get-better-with-time-says-abhishek-nayar-on-bowli.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The initial match of the series, held in Thiruvananthapuram, presented difficult conditions for the Indian bowlers. Despite these circumstances, Nayar has chosen to publicly back the unit, suggesting that their performance will evolve as the series progresses.</p><p>This endorsement from Nayar indicates a belief in the inherent capabilities and potential for adaptation within the Indian bowling attack. Such support from within the cricket fraternity can be crucial for team morale, particularly after a demanding start to a series.</p><p>The focus now shifts to the upcoming second ODI, where the Indian bowling contingent will have another opportunity to demonstrate their prowess. Conditions and pitch characteristics often play a significant role in bowling performances, and adjustments are frequently necessary.</p><p>Nayar's statement suggests that the team management and coaching staff are looking at the bigger picture, anticipating improvements as the bowlers gain more rhythm and familiarity with the playing conditions against West Indies.</p><p>What to watch: India's bowling adjustments in the second ODI against West Indies.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately reflects the source summary regarding Abhishek Nayar's comments.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
