     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · Times of India · 2026-09-30 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>J&amp;K Parties, Excluding BJP, Criticize Officials Over Anti-Statehood Letter to Assembly</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#politics</span> <span>#Jammu and Kashmir</span> <span>#statehood</span> <span>#governance</span></div>
       <p>Political parties in Jammu and Kashmir, with the exception of the Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP), have voiced strong criticism against officials concerning a letter submitted to the assembly. The document in question reportedly expresses opposition to the restoration of statehood for the region, sparking widespread condemnation.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-30/jk-parties-excluding-bjp-criticize-officials-over-anti-state.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>This collective reaction from various political factions underscores a significant point of contention within the local political arena. The parties have expressed their disapproval, interpreting the letter as a move that works against the long-standing demand for statehood, a key issue for many in Jammu and Kashmir.</p><p>The incident highlights the persistent advocacy for statehood restoration among a majority of political entities in the union territory. The criticism from these parties suggests a united front on this specific matter, emphasizing their shared concerns regarding the region's administrative and constitutional future.</p><p>While the precise contents of the letter or the identities of the officials involved were not detailed, the summary indicates a clear and robust objection to its implications. The BJP's exclusion from this chorus of criticism points to a differing perspective on the issue, illustrating a clear division within the political discourse surrounding Jammu and Kashmir's status. This divergence reflects the complex and often polarized nature of political discussions in the region.</p><p>What to watch: Ongoing political discourse and any official responses to the criticism regarding the letter.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately reflects the source summary without adding extraneous or unbacked details.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/jk-parties-barring-bjp-blast-top-officials-for-letter-to-assembly-against-statehood-resolution/articleshow/134601139.cms" rel="noopener">Times of India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
