     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-09-30 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Kamchatka Earthquake Produced High Local Tsunami but Smaller Pacific Waves</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Kamchatka</span> <span>#Earthquake</span> <span>#Tsunami</span> <span>#Geophysics</span></div>
       <p>A magnitude 8.8 earthquake struck parts of Kamchatka’s coast in Russia in 2025, triggering a tsunami that surged nearly 20 meters, or 65 feet, above sea level near the source. Despite the significant height of the waves in the immediate vicinity, the tsunami’s impact across the Pacific Ocean was notably smaller than that produced by the region’s historic 9.0 earthquake in 1952. </p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-30/kamchatka-earthquake-produced-high-local-tsunami-but-smaller.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Scientists are examining why the tsunami climbed so high locally yet had a comparatively modest impact far away. The discrepancy highlights the complex dynamics of earthquake mechanics and wave propagation across ocean basins. </p><p>While the 2025 event generated severe local flooding along the Kamchatka coast, the energy dispersed differently as it traveled across the Pacific. This stands in contrast to the 1952 quake, which generated larger waves over long distances. </p><p>Researchers continue to analyze the specific conditions of the 2025 event to understand the factors limiting the far-field tsunami amplitude. </p><p>What to watch: Further scientific analysis of the 2025 earthquake’s mechanics and its implications for long-range tsunami forecasting.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article provides a faithful summary of the scientific scenario provided.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-09-kamchatka-giant-earthquake-smaller-tsunami.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
