       <h1>Molineux, Hamilton ruled out of Bangladesh ODIs; McGrath to captain</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Cricket</span> <span>#ODI Series</span> <span>#Australia Women's Cricket</span> <span>#Player Injuries</span></div>
       <p>Australia's women's cricket team faces a significant challenge ahead of their three-match One Day International (ODI) series against Bangladesh, with key players Sophie Molineux and Lucy Hamilton officially ruled out due to injury. The absence of these two talents will undoubtedly test the squad's depth and tactical flexibility as they prepare for the upcoming contests.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-30/molineux-hamilton-ruled-out-of-bangladesh-odis-mcgrath-to-ca.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>In a crucial leadership development, Tahlia McGrath has been confirmed as the captain for the series. McGrath, a seasoned all-rounder, will step into the captaincy role, bringing her experience and strategic acumen to the forefront. Her leadership will be vital as the team navigates the series without two of its established players.</p><p>Molineux and Hamilton's injuries mean that selectors will need to adjust the team composition, potentially offering opportunities for other players to demonstrate their capabilities on the international stage. This situation often provides a chance for emerging talents to impress and solidify their positions within the squad.</p><p>The three-match ODI series against Bangladesh represents an important fixture in the international calendar, and the team will be keen to perform strongly despite the setbacks. All eyes will be on McGrath's captaincy and how the team adapts to the changes forced upon them by these unfortunate injuries.</p><p>What to watch: How the Australian squad adapts to key injuries under new captain Tahlia McGrath.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article is a faithful representation of the provided source summary.</p>
