     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-09-30 · editor 6/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Mysterious oarfish's fins inspire large underwater robot design</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#robotics</span> <span>#marine biology</span> <span>#engineering</span> <span>#oarfish</span></div>
       <p>Roboticist Rob Shepherd has drawn inspiration from the natural world to develop a novel design for large underwater robots. His research focuses on the unusual swimming system of the oarfish, a meters-long marine creature renowned for its distinctive ribbon-like dorsal fin. This fin propels the fish through water using a unique wave-like motion.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-30/mysterious-oarfishs-fins-inspire-large-underwater-robot-desi.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The primary goal behind this biomimetic design is to enable underwater robots to swim silently. Such stealth is crucial for avoiding the startling of sea creatures, allowing for less intrusive observation and interaction within marine ecosystems. The oarfish's method of propulsion, characterized by the undulating movement of its dorsal fin, offers an effective model for achieving this quiet navigation.</p><p>Shepherd considered this unusual system specifically for its potential to facilitate silent movement. By mimicking the oarfish's ribbon-like fin and its wave-like propulsion, engineers aim to create robots capable of moving through the water with minimal disturbance. This approach signifies a significant step towards developing more environmentally conscious and effective tools for underwater research and exploration. The integration of natural biological designs into advanced robotics could pave the way for new capabilities in marine science.</p><p>What to watch: Further advancements in biomimetic underwater robotics designed for silent marine observation.</p>
       
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-09-mysterious-oarfish-fins-large-underwater.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
