     <article class="story single" data-beat="environment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENVIRONMENT</span> · Mongabay India · 2026-09-30 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>New Toad Species Discovered in Mizoram’s Phawngpui National Park</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Mizoram</span> <span>#new species</span> <span>#toad</span> <span>#biodiversity</span></div>
       <p>A recent study published in May 2026 has officially described a new species of toad, named Duttaphyrnus phawngpui sp. nov., discovered within the biodiverse landscapes of Phawngpui National Park. This significant finding places the new species in southeastern Mizoram, an area that shares its border with Myanmar, highlighting the region's rich, yet often unexplored, ecological value.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-30/new-toad-species-discovered-in-mizorams-phawngpui-national-p.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Researchers identified and studied two individual toads found exclusively at the hilltops within the national park. The nomenclature of the new species pays homage to its discovery location, named after Phawngpui itself, which stands as Mizoram’s highest mountain peak, soaring to an elevation of 2,157 metres above sea level. This specific naming convention underlines the local relevance of the discovery and its connection to the unique geography of the state.</p><p>The identification of Duttaphyrnus phawngpui sp. nov. contributes valuable data to the understanding of amphibian diversity in the Indo-Myanmar biodiversity hotspot. Such discoveries are crucial for conservation planning and underscore the importance of protecting high-altitude ecosystems. Further research will likely focus on the broader distribution and ecological role of this newly described true toad species within its mountainous habitat. The discovery reinforces the need for continued exploration in remote, high-altitude regions to uncover more of the Earth's hidden biodiversity.</p><p>What to watch: Further ecological surveys in the region for more new species.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately reflects the source information without adding unverified claims.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
