       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · Al Jazeera · 2026-09-30 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>OpenAI launches ‘dots,’ personal AI assistant ‘built to handle everything’</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#AI</span> <span>#OpenAI</span> <span>#technology</span> <span>#personal assistant</span></div>
       <p>OpenAI, a leading artificial intelligence giant, has officially launched its new personal AI assistant, named 'dots'. This new offering enters a highly competitive market, positioning itself as a direct rival to established and emerging platforms from other tech titans.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-30/openai-launches-dots-personal-ai-assistant-built-to-handle-e.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Described as an AI assistant &quot;built to handle everything,&quot; 'dots' aims to integrate comprehensively into users' daily lives, streamlining various tasks and interactions. The launch signifies OpenAI's continued expansion beyond its initial generative AI tools, moving deeper into personalized user-facing applications. This strategic move places 'dots' in direct competition with Meta's 'Muse' and Google's 'Gemini Spark', both of which are also vying for dominance in the burgeoning personal AI assistant sector.</p><p>The introduction of 'dots' intensifies the rivalry among major technology companies in the artificial intelligence space. As these firms continue to innovate, the focus appears to be shifting towards creating more versatile and integrated AI solutions designed to enhance personal productivity and digital interaction. The competition is expected to drive further advancements in AI capabilities and user experience.</p><p>What to watch: How 'dots' differentiates itself from competitors and its initial user adoption rates.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article faithfully represents the provided source summary.</p>
