     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · Livemint Money · 2026-09-30 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Proving Inherited Gold Ownership: A Taxpayer's Guide</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Income Tax</span> <span>#Inheritance</span> <span>#Gold</span> <span>#Tax Compliance</span></div>
       <p>For many ordinary savers and taxpayers, inherited family gold holds significant sentimental and financial value. However, a recent ruling by the Kolkata Income Tax Appellate Tribunal highlights a crucial aspect of owning such assets: the need to prove ownership and source to the Income Tax Department if questioned.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-30/proving-inherited-gold-ownership-a-taxpayers-guide.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The tribunal's decision, made in favour of an assessee in a specific case, underscores that merely possessing inherited jewellery is not always sufficient. What is paramount is the ability to provide credible and consistent evidence to support claims about how the jewellery was acquired, particularly if it was inherited. This means that while the tax department may accept reasonable explanations, these explanations must be backed by tangible proof wherever possible.</p><p>For an ordinary individual, this implies a need for diligence. If you inherit gold or jewellery, it's wise to maintain any documentation that can attest to its origin, such as a will, gift deed, or even old purchase receipts if available. In the absence of formal documents, other forms of consistent evidence, like family records or affidavits, might become important. The core message is clear: preparedness and proper documentation are key to avoiding potential disputes with tax authorities regarding inherited assets.</p><p>What to watch: The types of evidence accepted by tax authorities for inherited assets.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article provides a helpful and accurate summary of the tribunal ruling and its implications for taxpayers.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.livemint.com/money/personal-finance/inherited-family-gold-how-to-prove-ownership-and-source-of-jewellery-to-income-tax-department-11790771251419.html" rel="noopener">Livemint Money</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
