     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · The Verge · 2026-09-30 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Tech Leaders Commit to AI Self-Regulation Under New Accord</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#AI</span> <span>#Technology</span> <span>#Policy</span> <span>#Regulation</span></div>
       <p>President Trump has unveiled the details of a new agreement focused on the self-regulation of artificial intelligence technology. The accord, officially titled the Joint Commitment On Frontier Responsibilities, establishes a framework for tech executives to manage the development and safety of their AI systems.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-30/tech-leaders-commit-to-ai-self-regulation-under-new-accord.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The agreement was shared publicly by presidential advisor and tech founder David Sacks. According to the document, top executives from leading technology companies have signed onto the commitment. The terms of the deal are described as &quot;morally binding,&quot; placing the responsibility for AI safety directly on the participating firms rather than through government-mandated legislation.</p><p>While the scope of the commitments is broad, the initiative marks a significant shift in how the administration intends to oversee the rapidly evolving AI sector. By prioritizing industry self-policing, the deal aims to balance innovation with safety concerns, relying on the participating leaders to uphold the standards outlined in the document.</p><p>What to watch: Whether these voluntary commitments lead to measurable changes in AI safety practices across the industry.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately reflects the provided source material without adding external information.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
