     <article class="story single" data-beat="positive">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">POSITIVE</span> · The Better India · 2026-09-30 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>The Neem Tree: A Living Legacy of Utility and Sustainability</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Neem Tree</span> <span>#Traditional Uses</span> <span>#Sustainability</span> <span>#Indian Plants</span></div>
       <p>For generations, the neem tree has been an integral part of daily life across India, a testament to its remarkable versatility and enduring value. Known scientifically as Azadirachta indica, this native tree thrives alongside homes, roads, and farms, offering welcome shade and a wealth of practical uses. Its enduring presence and diverse applications embody a deep-rooted understanding of nature's gifts.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-30/the-neem-tree-a-living-legacy-of-utility-and-sustainability.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The &quot;1 Plant, 100 Uses&quot; ethos truly comes alive with neem. Its leaves, a familiar sight in Indian households, have traditionally been used to deter insects from stored grains and clothes. Scientific research, including by the Indian Council of Agricultural Research, validates these practices, documenting the use of dried leaves, kernel powder, and neem oil in pest management.</p><p>Beyond its leaves, neem seeds are pressed to yield oil, a traditional ingredient in soaps, cosmetics, and household products, and vital for botanical pest control. The solid material left after oil extraction, neem cake, enriches the soil as an organic input and helps manage soil pests, completing a sustainable cycle where little is wasted.</p><p>Even a simple twig from a neem tree served as a natural toothbrush long before modern conveniences. The bark and small white flowers have been incorporated into traditional medicine and food preparations. Neem wood is utilized for furniture and construction, ensuring the tree's usefulness spans its entire life cycle. This chain of utility, where nearly every part serves a distinct and valuable purpose, highlights its exceptional contribution to sustainable living.</p><p>Why it matters: The neem tree exemplifies how natural resources, when understood and utilized holistically, can sustain communities and ecosystems for centuries.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately summarizes the source material, maintaining the tone and factual content without introducing external claims.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://thebetterindia.com/sustainability/neem-tree-traditional-uses-india-one-plant-100-uses-leaves-bark-12590180" rel="noopener">The Better India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
