     <article class="story single" data-beat="business">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · Economic Times · 2026-09-30 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Trump Just Fired Starting Gun on New War with China</h1>
       
       <p>The Economic Times published a story with the headline that former President Donald Trump has &quot;fired the starting gun&quot; on a new war with China. The piece, as summarized, offers no additional details about the alleged conflict, such as any official statements, specific actions, or policy measures. No dates, locations, or figures are provided, and the summary does not include quotes from government officials, military analysts, or diplomatic sources. Consequently, the article remains limited to the headline's claim without corroborating evidence or context. Readers are left without information on whether the headline reflects a policy announcement, a rhetorical statement, or a speculative report. The lack of further data means the story cannot be evaluated for its strategic implications, potential economic impact, or regional reactions. Until more substantive reporting emerges, the claim stands as an unelaborated headline that raises questions about its source and verification. </p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-30/trump-just-fired-starting-gun-on-new-war-with-china.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>What to watch: Any follow‑up statements from the White House, the State Department or Chinese officials that clarify the situation.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately reflects the lack of substance in the provided source summary.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
